//! Characterization oracle for the baseline stroke normalization `desP(d) -> serP(path)` (G14/G22/G13, leaf
//! L01-C of #2107). It owns no production code: it pins, as literals, what the shipped baseline does to open and
//! closed vectors, stroke and paint fields, pressure ribbons, `paramShape` and `pathFx`, so that the later record,
//! codec, mapper and render leaves (L01-D onward) can assert semantics against fixed facts instead of re-deriving
//! them.
//!
//! `fixtures/object-vector-serp-desp/oracle.txt` was generated by `tools/gen.cjs` (kept outside the repository) from
//! commit fbe4b8a33e6d5a229f014154df2ba21988a9fabc: `colorHex8`, `applyBrushKeyline`, `_r3`, `serP` and `desP` of
//! `src/js/app.js` run verbatim in a Node vm over the shipped `src/paper-full.min.js` (real Paper `Path`, `Segment`,
//! `Point`, colors). Every `case` row holds the literal input dictionary (`in`), the optional third `desP` argument
//! (`op`), the state of the rebuilt Paper item (`paper`), `serP` of that item (`out`) and the same pair applied again
//! to `out` (`out2`). Output objects omit undefined members; the `serp-keys` meta row lists every key `serP` can
//! return, in order. The value grammar is in `object_vector_oracle_value.rs`.
//!
//! This file checks that the oracle is complete and well formed; the literal pins are in `..._tables.rs` and are
//! compared by `..._pins.rs`; `..._controls.rs` proves that each check fails when the oracle is damaged.

use value::{parse_value, Value};

